yoyo config bogus no longer claims it needs an interactive session. It now says the word is an unknown config subcommand and lists the forms that work. The change is committed as e8356c58, and build, test, clippy, fmt and tests/harness_logic.sh all pass.

What the built binary prints now:

| Input | stderr | exit |
|---|---|---|
| config bogus | unknown config subcommand: bogus  + Try: yoyo config show, yoyo config get <key> (work from the shell); inside yoyo: /config edit, /config set | 2 |
| config shwo | same, plus (did you mean show?) | 2 |
| config mcp | unknown (the REPL doesn't know /config mcp either) | 2 |
| config set / config edit | requires an interactive session: start yoyo and use /config set + the shell Try line | 2 |
| config show, config get | unchanged | 0 / 2 |

How it's built:
• The wording comes from a new function, config_shell_refusal, in src/commands_config_get.rs. It takes the list of subcommands from CONFIG_SUBCOMMANDS and the did-you-mean from the existing closest_match helper.
• dispatch_sub.rs just prints what that function returns. Its line count is unchanged at 2683.
• A round-trip test reads the config branch in dispatch_sub.rs. It checks that the branch handles exactly the shell forms the message suggests, and that every other real subcommand gets the interactive wording. As a check that the test can fail, I broke the list on purpose: 3 tests went red, and I restored it in the same command.

Two existing integration tests changed. config_unknown_subcommand_refusal_exits_two was asserting the false message itself ("config zzz requires an interactive session"). config_set_and_edit_refusals_exit_two was asserting the old set/edit wording. Both now check the full new strings, and a new binary-level test covers config bogus and config shwo. docs/src/usage/commands.md is updated too.

Other commands with the same message: I grepped src/ for "requires/needs an interactive session" and found 4 refusals, then ran each with the built binary. Only config was wrong:
• extended bogus treats "bogus" as a task description, so saying it needs the REPL is true.
• goal check and lint fix really are REPL-only, and goal bogus / lint bogus already say "unknown subcommand".

So 4 of 4 checked, nothing left over, and I filed no follow-up issue.
